\date{2024-07-26}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Equivalence}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\import{common}

\p{
  A map #{{f : X \to Y}} is an \concept{equivalence} if any of the following logically equivalent propositional conditions hold:
  \ul{
    \li{\strong{Biinvertibility:} #{f} has a section and a retraction. I.e., the following type is inhabited
    ##{\begin{aligned}\operatorname{\texttt{is-biinvertible}} f \defeq &\left(\sigma-type{g : Y \to X}{(f \o g \sim \id)}\right)\times\\ &\left(\sigma-type{g' : Y \to X}{(g' \o f\sim \id)}\right)\mathrlap{.}
    \end{aligned}}}

    \li{\strong{Coherent invertibility:} There is a map #{g : Y \to X} that is both a section and retraction to #{f} with witnessing homotopies #{S} and #{R} respectively, such that these witnesses satisfy either of the following higher coherences
    ##{Sf \sim fR \quad\text{or}\quad gS \sim Rg\mathrlap{.}}}

    \li{\strong{Contractible map:} #{f} has contractible fibers.}

    \li{\strong{Surjective embedding:} #{f} is a surjective embedding. This characterization of equivalences extends to every [[hott-000I]] #{L}. The map #{f} is an equivalence iff it is #{L}-connected and #{L}-local. }

  }
}
